we're joined now by the Labour mayor of  Greater Manchester Andy Burnham thank  you very much for being on the show this  morning  well tourism is written to her  colleagues saying that history will  judge them all for the part they've  played in the brexit process so how do  you think that MPs and Westminster will  be judged by history for what they're  getting onto quite badly if things don't  change this letter actually sophie sheds  I think a bit of light on what is now  the deepest political crisis of our  lifetimes if you read it and I encourage  all people to do that it basically tells  you that it's all about the Tory Party  or should I say the to Tory parties that  we've got because there was a government  minister last week saying that the ERG  are effectively a party within a party  what she says in the letter is that she  spent the last two and a half years  trying to bring these factions together  and I think that in the end of the day  is the problem they can't be brought  together I did spend 16 years in  Parliament as you said and I sat through  all of those debates and where Bill  Clark Ash and Kenneth Clarke were just  arguing that there is no agreement  between these people and that's the  Prime Minister's failing she spent all  of her efforts to try and do that we're  actually all along there's probably a  deal that could get through Parliament  and I believe that's a deal with a  customs union as part of it as Jeremy  Corbyn has has suggested so it's a very  frustrating situation but now we are in  very perilous waters indeed you see this  is what I don't understand because it's  easy to point the finger at the finger  at the Conservative Party isn't it you  say that there is the deal to be done  with a customs union that Labour could  support but there is a customs union in  the withdrawal agreement and the future  relationship hasn't been decided yet so  why is labour risking no deal and not  backing the Prime Minister's deal I  think this is where Europe needs to  accept some of the blame because they've  set up withdrawal agreement that is very  detailed and quite punitive in some ways  but we have a political declaration  alongside it that's all very vague and  that just doesn't work as far as I'm  concerned you need to know what that  relationship is going to be if we're  going to accept all of the terms in the  withdrawal agreement but I do come back  to my main point  Sophie I don't believe the Tory party  can be united on this particular topic  at the moment it feels like the  Conservative Party is holding the whole  country to ransom and that can't be  allowed to carry on I think there is a  deal that could be put to Parliament and  the Prime Minister needs to do that  regardless of what damage it might do  internally to the Tory party without  that I don't see how we can move forward  I'm also really keen to try and pin you  down on your position on a second  referendum because I'm a little bit  confused by some of the things that  you've said about it and in the past so  in September you said as a last resort  when everything else has failed then and  only then should we consider holding a  second vote but then a month earlier in  August you said my frustration with  those leaping to a second referendum is  that it further inflamed the idea of an  arrogant political class which isn't  listening so at what point do you know  campaigners for a second referendum go  from arrogant to responsible no those  two things are entirely compatible I was  born in back in August about the dangers  of a second referendum and I still  believe that it would be fraught with  problems Sophie it would cause lasting  division in the country I think for a  generation  so those comments stand when I then gave  a more detailed speech in September I  said it should be the very last resort  the only thing that I think is worse is  a No Deal brexit so in that scenario  when we are at that Cliff edge at that  point I would support a second  referendum and I think actually that was  broadly the position that was then  adopted by the Labour Party at its  annual conference so I remain in that  position today I do believe we should do  everything possible to honor the result  of the referendum and get on with a deal  a brexit deal that works for us the  customs union is crucial Greater  Manchester sends 60% of its exports to  Europe you know we cannot be in a  position where from the beginning of  April onwards those businesses are  facing barriers and tariffs we can't  because that will damage those  businesses it will result in jobs being  lost so that is why we're saying we need  that working customs arrangement and I'm  afraid the time has  for the Prime Minister to part company  with those hardliners in her party  because there is not a deal that she can  do with them that will be acceptable to  the rest of the country so just to be  clear if you're saying that the only  thing that's preferable to a second  referendum is No Deal  does that mean that you'd accept the  Prime Minister's deal above a second  referendum no I don't think we should be  forced to accept a suboptimal deal you  know with all of the uncertainties  around the situation in Northern Ireland  that no I don't think we should be  required to accept that there is a  better deal I believe that can be done  and one that could unite Parliament and  it's a deal that has a customs  arrangement as part of it I think there  is a majority in parliament for that but  the Prime Minister won't put that  proposal to Parliament for fear of the  damage it will do internally to the  conservative and unionist party now this  is the problem Sophie this is the point  that we've arrived at you know all the  Tory leaders in the past have taken on  these hardliners and I'm afraid it's  time for the Prime Minister to either do  that or let Parliament take control of  this situation okay and we talked a lot  about and conservative divisions over  brexit and which are pretty clear there  are also labor genuinely held labor  divisions over brexit as well there's  also a lot of rumors going around about  a new party forming about some MPs  perhaps splitting away from labor and  resigning the whip do you think it's  inevitable that that's going to happen  no I don't think it's inevitable the  colleagues that I worked with in  Parliament have a deep loyalty to the  Labour Party so I think at the moment  it's more speculation than than reality  but you're right it's a very turbulent  time in British politics Sophie and  we're living through a degree of  uncertainty I don't think any of us have  ever experienced and that there has to  be a realignment I guess coming out of  all of this you know in these parts I  think the big thing that people felt  when they vote in the referendum wasn't  just that they wanted a different  relationship with Europe they wanted the  UK Parliament to work differently to  look after parts of the country like  this there have always been neglected in  our London centric system so I think  coming out of this we do need to see  some Realignment not necessarily within  the political parties but actually  within the country I don't think we can  carry on with this over centralized  political system as I say that's very  London centric and leaves large parts of  the country without the investment that  it needs and that is so obvious when you  come to transport or pier and the  difference between transport here and  what people experience in the south so  you know these are the kind of issues I  think that are going to need to be  addressed when we finally come through  this brexit debate and we must come  through it so because we experienced a  real blight here on day to day decision  making we're still waiting in Greater  Manchester for the government to give us  the compensation for the cost of the  mall and fires back in the summer I mean  this isn't the normal running of UK  government you know things are  completely on hold at the moment and we  can't carry on like this because we're  drifting into very dangerous territory  indeed if you are and you know I mean if  things are as bad as you paint a picture  of them  why isn't labour doing better in the  polls is it the leadership that's a  problem I think we're in a position here  where as you say there's there's no  unity if you like in the two main  parties so I think the country are  saying through those polls that they're  not sure where both of the main parties  are you know there's a poll today that  says they're neck-and-neck I think that  reflects that fact that there's a  confused debate going on in the Tory  Party and a confused debate going on  within the labour party that's what the  opinion polls are saying right now I  think we do need to come through this  period and get back to focusing on  domestic issues that have been very  neglected I would say in the last in the  last two years people are living through  really tough times but there's not a  sense at the moment that people in  Parliament are understanding that or  doing anything about it I think the  level of disconnect Sophie between  Parliament and the people has never been  greater than it is right now okay well  let's talk about some of those domestic  issues shall we and homelessness is  clearly a huge issue for many towns and  cities across the country and you said  that you would eradicate homelessness in  Greater Manchester by 2020 you've only  got a year to go are you going to do it  I didn't quite say that I said I would  end rough sleeping and there's a  difference between those two things but  I'm not trying to do the politician  thing and sidestep well we recently  recorded the first fall in levels of  people sleeping roughing Greater munch  there for eight years so we believe  we've turned the tide and things are now  heading in the right direction and one  of the reasons for that is because we  are the only city region in the country  that through this winter has sought to  give a place for every person sleeping  rough to go every night of the week and  it's had really impressive results it's  not a perfect scheme but already almost  500 people have moved from that stable  provision within those shelters to a  fixed address and this is my frustration  Sophie to be honest if the government  were to focus a little more on  bread-and-butter issues like that I  think we could make more progress more  quickly instead we saw a universal  credit rolled out here over the course  of the last year and that only added to  the problem that I'm facing but but  nevertheless no I don't back away from  it in my view in this day and age in a  country as wealthy as this we should be  able to put a roof over every head every  night of the week and I don't believe  that perhaps some people who are  successful in society other people have  to sleep in doorways this in my view is  a national scandal we have the means to  fix it we should get on and fix it you  know the need for safe affordable  housing in my view should be a human  right in UK law and I believe those are  the some of the changes we need to see  to bring this country back together once  this brexit debate is over and just  finally what we've got you and Donald  Trump who's called today on the UK and  other European and countries to take  back Islamic state fighters or else and  put them on trial otherwise in his words  they're going to permeate Europe now  there are dozens of jihadis from  Manchester who are currently unaccounted  for  so what do you think should happen do  they need to come back to the UK and  also how do you deal with jihadi wives  such as shamina Begum  well you won't be surprised here that  not for the first time I don't agree  with Donald Trump nah I don't think it  should be automatically people come back  I I read all the details of that story  last last week and if somebody leaves  this country and joins a group that is  actively plotting to to harm and kill  people in this country it surely can't  be right that then people come and walk  straight back in when it's convenient  and yeah I know that some of those  caught up in the Manchester attack feel  very strongly about this situation  because of the echos with the situation  that happened there so no I don't  believe that Donald Trump is right about  this I understand the legal complexities  that the government will be facing but I  would think the vast majority of people  here in Greater Manchester's country and  if you've walked away and joined a group  plotting to harm us that you don't come  back and the thing was with this the  lack of remorse that was what I think I  found really shocking and I'm sure other  people would find shocking too so it's a  difficult situation but no I don't  there's any guarantees that that is what  happens okay and do you buy them thanks  very much for being on the program

